<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#malay-language">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malay language</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Malay language is an Austronesian language spoken in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> and much of maritime South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. Its earliest inscriptions, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-malay">Old Malay</a>, date from the period of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>. The spread of Malay as a language of trade and administration is among Srivijaya's lasting legacies, and it is the basis of modern Malaysian and Indonesian.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malay-language.jpg" alt="Malay language" width="320" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kerina yin at Malay Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hukum_Kanun_Melaka_(manuskrip_asal)_2.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Historical linguists agree that the Malayic homeland was probably in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>. A form called Proto-Malayic was spoken there by at least 1000 BCE and is argued to be the ancestor of all later Malayic languages. Its own ancestor, Proto-Malayo-Polynesian, began to break up by at least 2000 BCE, possibly through the southward expansion of Austronesian peoples from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-malay">Old Malay</a>, believed to be the ancestor of Classical Malay, was influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>. The earliest known stone inscription in Old Malay, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kedukan-bukit-inscription">Kedukan Bukit inscription</a>, was found on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a> and is dated 1 May 683, written in the Pallava variety of the Grantha alphabet. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, a maritime empire based on Sumatra from the 7th to the 11th centuries, spread Old Malay through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a> and Archipelago, where it served as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a> of traders in ports and marketplaces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terengganu-inscription-stone">Terengganu Inscription Stone</a>, dated approximately to 1303 CE and written in Jawi script, is considered the earliest evidence of Classical Malay. Its ruler urges his subjects to uphold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and sets out ten basic laws. Classical Malay came into wide use as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a> under the Malacca Sultanate (1402 to 1511), a hub of trade and Islamic learning. Islamic literature brought a large infusion of Arabic vocabulary, alongside continued influence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and Tamil.</p><p>After the Portuguese captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> in 1511 the court re-established itself as the Johor Sultanate and kept Classical Malay as its literary and administrative language, while literary centres dispersed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, Makassar, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Moluccas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>. In the 19th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and British, divided by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-dutch-treaty-of-1824">Anglo-Dutch Treaty of 1824</a>, both used Malay as a tool of centralisation and modernisation.</p><p>Before Arabic script arrived, Malay was written in the Pallava, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Kawi</a> and Rencong scripts. From the era of the Kingdom of Pasai and the golden age of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>, Jawi gradually replaced them. From the 17th century, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and British influence, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> script <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a> gradually replaced Jawi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Malay is an Austronesian pluricentric macrolanguage, native to several islands of Maritime Southeast Asia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a>. It is an official language of Brunei, Malaysia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, where its standard form is Standard Malay, and Indonesian, an autonomous standard, is the official language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. It is spoken as a first language by about 80 million people and as a first or second language by close to 300 million. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> script is official in Malaysia, Singapore and Indonesia, and Jawi is co-official with it in Brunei and Malaysia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Malay language” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…thought to have been revised for the Johor court in 1612, and the Bugis-Malay court of Riau-Lingga became a centre of Malay letters in the nineteenth century, producing Raja Ali Haji's Tuhfat al-Nafis and grammars that helped standardise the <b>Malay language</b> from which modern Malay and Indonesian developed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Old Malay, used in Srivijaya's inscriptions and spread as a language of commerce across its network, was an ancestor of the <b>Malay language</b> that later became one of the most widely spoken languages of Southeast Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/srivijaya-empire">Srivijaya Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The name Malayu revived later in the Dharmasraya kingdom and spread to the <b>Malay language</b> and people.</blockquote><a href="/state/melayu-kingdom">Melayu Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
